CORPUS += [
    # ORBITAL PROPAGATION (additional)
    {"id":"sgp4-impl-001","domain":"orbital_propagation","title":"SGP4 Implementation Details","source":"Vallado SGP4 code; Spacetrack Report 3","text":"SGP4 implementation steps: (1) Initialize from TLE: convert mean motion to rad/s, compute semi-major axis from n0 using Kepler's third law, compute Earth constants. (2) Apply secular perturbations: compute secular rates for M, ω, Ω using J2; compute drag-related secular terms using B*. (3) Propagate to desired time t: compute updated mean elements. (4) Solve Kepler's equation: M → E (eccentric anomaly) via Newton-Raphson iteration; E → ν (true anomaly). (5) Compute position/velocity in orbital plane. (6) Transform to TEME frame. Critical implementation detail: TLE mean motion is already corrected for J2 secular effects; it is NOT equal to sqrt(μ/a^3). The recovery of semi-major axis from TLE n requires solving n_TLE = n_Kepler × (1 + K1/p^2 × (1 - 1.5*sin^2(i))) for a, where K1 involves J2 and other constants. Source code available from Celestrak (Vallado's C++/Python) and AGI's GPL code."},
    {"id":"sgp4-reference-frames-001","domain":"orbital_propagation","title":"TEME to J2000 Transformation","source":"Vallado, Fundamentals of Astrodynamics 4th ed.","text":"Converting SGP4 TEME output to J2000 (or GCRF) requires accounting for Earth's precession and nutation. TEME uses the true equator (of date) and mean equinox, while J2000 uses the mean equator and equinox of J2000.0 epoch. Transformation: r_J2000 = R3(-GAST) × R_EOD where EOD = equator of date. Full transformation: (1) TEME → TOD (True of Date): apply mean-to-true nutation correction R_nut; (2) TOD → MOD (Mean of Date): apply nutation; (3) MOD → J2000: apply precession matrix. For most operational purposes, the simplified TEME → J2000 transform using Greenwich Apparent Sidereal Time (GAST) and a simple nutation correction (Δψ, Δε) is sufficient, accurate to ~1 km. Tools: Vallado's MATLAB/Python code, astropy, poliastro (Python), Orekit (Java)."},
    {"id":"orbit-determination-iod-001","domain":"orbital_propagation","title":"Initial Orbit Determination Methods","source":"Vallado; Tapley Schutz Born","text":"Initial Orbit Determination (IOD) determines an orbit from sparse observations. Gauss method (angles-only, 3 observations): uses the f and g series to relate position vectors at three times. Solves an 8th-degree polynomial for the geocentric range at middle observation. Requires observations separated by <2-3% of orbital period. Laplace method: differentiates the line-of-sight vector series. More stable than Gauss for certain geometries. Gibbs method (3 position vectors): r2 = (r1×r2×r3) cross-products. Requires known position at 3 times (from range observations). Double-r (Gooding) method: iterative, handles a wider range of geometries. Observation types: (1) optical (topocentric angles RA, Dec or Az, El) — common for objects in GEO/deep space; (2) radar range and range-rate — common for LEO objects; (3) combined angle and range. IOD is followed by differential correction to refine the orbit using all available observations."},
    {"id":"tle-limitations-001","domain":"orbital_propagation","title":"TLE Accuracy Limitations","source":"Celestrak documentation; Vallado","text":"Two-Line Elements have several fundamental limitations: (1) Epoch accuracy: TLEs represent the best-fit mean elements at epoch, not instantaneous osculating elements. Cannot be used with a general numerical propagator — must use SGP4. (2) Short shelf life: LEO TLEs typically accurate for 1-7 days; debris TLEs may be 10-30 days old. (3) Covariance not included: TLE format has no uncertainty information. (4) Mean element theory: SGP4 uses a simplified force model; large residuals from unmodeled forces (high solar activity, maneuvers, attitude changes). (5) No maneuver information: TLEs are recomputed after maneuvers but don't include maneuver data. (6) Classification: some TLEs are available at unclassified level while higher-accuracy data remains classified. (7) Single-point: TLE represents state at a single epoch, not an ephemeris. For high-precision applications (conjunction analysis, rendezvous), operators use precision orbit determination with full force models."},
    # CONJUNCTION ANALYSIS (additional)
    {"id":"conjunction-algorithms-001","domain":"conjunction_analysis","title":"Pc Computation Methods","source":"Foster Estes 1992; Alfano 2005; Patera 2001","text":"Multiple methods exist for computing probability of collision (Pc): (1) 2D Chan/Foster method: most common, projects 3D PDF onto encounter plane (B-plane), integrates 2D Gaussian over circle. Fast, accurate for short encounter duration. (2) 3D methods: integrate over full 3D uncertainty ellipsoid. Required when encounter duration is non-negligible. (3) Monte Carlo: sample from uncertainty distribution, count fraction of realizations resulting in collision. Accurate but computationally expensive. (4) Maximum Pc methods: compute upper bound on Pc regardless of conjunction geometry. Useful when covariance data is poor. (5) Time-varying Pc: accounts for covariance evolution during encounter. Alfano (2005) extends Chan method for arbitrary geometry. Patera (2001) derives exact 3D formulation. Key computational issue: 2D method can fail when objects' uncertainty ellipsoids overlap significantly. Maximum Pc (Pc_max) upper bound: Pc ≤ 1 - exp(-combined_volume / encounter_volume). Numerical integration libraries used: scipy.integrate.dblquad in Python, QUAD routines in MATLAB."},
    {"id":"conjunction-watch-001","domain":"conjunction_analysis","title":"Conjunction Watch and Warning Process","source":"US Space Command; NASA CARA; ESA SOCM","text":"US Space Command conjunction watch and warning process: (1) Routine screening: continuous conjunction screening of all cataloged objects. Objects passing within screening volume (5×25×25 km LEO) generate Conjunction Summary Message (CSM). (2) Watch issued: if CSM shows Pc ≥ 1e-5 within 7 days, a watch is issued. (3) Warning issued: Pc ≥ 1e-4 within 72 hours. (4) Maneuver evaluation: operator decides whether to maneuver. (5) Maneuver notification: operator notifies 18th SpCS to update screening after planned maneuver. (6) Post-maneuver verification: new CSMs generated with updated orbit. NASA CARA (Conjunction Assessment Risk Analysis): manages conjunction screening for NASA operational assets. Applies Pc threshold of 1e-5 for watches, 1e-4 for warnings. Uses precision orbit determination for NASA assets. ESA SOCM (Space Operations and Control Management): similar process for ESA assets. Coordination challenges: response time (CSMs may come <24h before TCA), maneuvering uncertainties, communication between operators."},
    {"id":"conjunction-geometry-001","domain":"conjunction_analysis","title":"Encounter Geometry and B-Plane","source":"Opik 1951; Greenberg 1982; Alfano 2005","text":"Conjunction geometry is characterized by the B-plane (aim point plane): the plane perpendicular to the relative velocity vector at TCA, passing through the center of the secondary object. Key B-plane parameters: B·T (transverse component of miss vector in B-plane), B·R (radial component), |B| (total miss distance = Mahalanobis distance × sigma). For the 2D Pc computation: the combined position covariance is projected onto the B-plane, creating a 2D error ellipse. The Pc integral is over the area of a circle (hard-body disk) relative to this ellipse. When the relative velocity is along-track (as in planar co-altitude conjunctions), the in-plane covariance dominates. When the relative velocity is cross-track (as in inclination-difference conjunctions), different components matter. Miss vector components: radial (R, toward Earth center), transverse (T, along velocity), normal (N, out of plane). CDM provides relative position in RTN frame. Encounter duration: typical LEO conjunction lasts 0.1-10 seconds of time within combined hard-body radius."},
    {"id":"conjunction-database-001","domain":"conjunction_analysis","title":"Conjunction Database and Statistics","source":"LeoLabs; 18th SpCS reports; ESA DISCOS","text":"Space conjunction statistics: Space-Track CDM volume: thousands of CSMs per day for all cataloged objects. For a single active satellite in LEO, typical event rate: 1-5 events per week at Pc > 1e-5; 0-2 events per month at Pc > 1e-4. ISS receives ~20-30 late notifications per year requiring evaluation; 3-5 maneuvers per year on average. Iridium-Cosmos collision (2009) was the first cataloged collision, generating 2,287 trackable fragments. Prior uncatalogued objects likely caused satellite anomalies. Statistics by orbit type: LEO has highest collision frequency due to density; MEO has fast-moving GPS orbit and long debris lifetimes; GEO has very slow relative velocities (10-100 m/s vs LEO 10+ km/s) but still debris collisions possible. LeoLabs data: of ~19,000 tracked LEO objects, ~4,000 are active payloads; 50+ high-priority conjunction events per day above 1e-5 Pc for entire LEO population."},
    # DEBRIS MITIGATION (additional)
    {"id":"debris-prevention-001","domain":"debris_mitigation","title":"On-Orbit Explosion Prevention","source":"IADC Guidelines; NASA 8719.14A","text":"On-orbit explosions are the primary source of large debris clouds. Causes: pressurized propellant tanks rupturing (thermal expansion, corrosion), battery overpressure (thermal runaway), chemical reactions in mixed propellants, intentional destruction. Historical examples: Chinese ASAT test (FY-1C, 2007): 3,500+ trackable fragments at 800 km altitude, 25-year cleanup time. US SM-3 ASAT (2008): low altitude, debris reentered within weeks. Cosmos 954 (1978): nuclear-powered Soviet RORSAT satellite reentered with nuclear reactor in Canada. Pegasus rocket body explosion (1996): generated hundreds of fragments. Prevention measures: passivation — required for all US government satellites since 1997; venting propellants, discharging batteries, depressurizing tanks. Results: passivation compliance has significantly reduced new explosion events. Historical explosions still dominate the debris population. Russian Zenit bodies and Chinese CZ rocket bodies are high-risk non-passivated objects."},
    {"id":"debris-removal-economics-001","domain":"debris_mitigation","title":"Active Debris Removal Economics and Prioritization","source":"ESA Clean Space; McKinsey debris study; Darren McKnight risk analysis","text":"Active Debris Removal (ADR) prioritization: Most dangerous objects are large (mass × collision cross-section), in stable orbits (high altitude = long lifetime), in crowded orbital bands (700-1000 km altitude). ESA analysis: ~50 objects need removal in first decade to stabilize LEO environment. Top candidates: Soviet/Russian Zenit-2 upper stages (~9,000 kg, at 1,000-1,400 km), SL-8 rocket bodies (~1,400 kg), Cosmos satellites. Chinese CZ-4 upper stages at 700-900 km. ADR mission costs: estimated $100-300M per mission for first-generation robotic capture. Economics: breakeven requires collision avoidance benefit exceeding ADR cost. With 27,000 tracked objects and growing, collision rates increase quadratically with population. US government insurance analogy: NASA estimates collision fragments worth $100M in damage per major collision event. Regulatory drivers: FCC 5-year deorbit rule (2022) changes the economics — new satellites must deorbit within 5 years, but the existing debris backlog remains unaddressed."},
    {"id":"debris-survey-001","domain":"debris_mitigation","title":"Space Debris Surveys and Measurements","source":"ESA Space Debris Environment Report 2023; NASA ODPO","text":"Space debris environment measurements: Haystack radar (Millstone Hill, MA): narrow beam, high sensitivity, used for statistical debris flux measurements. Haystack data is the primary source for debris population models 1-10 cm size range. EISCAT radar (Europe): similar capability in Europe. ESA telescope surveys: using 1 m telescopes at Tenerife and Calar Alto for GEO debris survey. MASTER (Meteoroid and Space Debris Terrestrial Environment Reference, ESA): population model based on radar measurements, optical surveys, and laboratory impact experiments. NASA ORDEM (Orbital Debris Engineering Model): similar capability, used for spacecraft shielding design. Key measurements: flux (objects/m^2/year as function of size, altitude, inclination). Sizes down to 3 mm measurable by Haystack. Key finding from surveys: breakup events have produced excess fragments in certain altitude/inclination bands. Flux at ISS altitude (408 km, 51.6°): approximately 10^-5 impacts/m^2/year for >1 cm objects."},
    # ORBITAL MECHANICS (additional)
    {"id":"orbital-perturbations-complete-001","domain":"orbital_mechanics","title":"Complete Perturbation Force Catalog","source":"Vallado; Montenbruck & Gill, Satellite Orbits","text":"Perturbation forces for precise satellite orbit determination (ranked by magnitude for LEO): (1) Earth gravity (geopotential): J2 >> J3 > J4 > J2,2 (tesseral) > other harmonics. EGM2008 gravitational model has degree/order 2190; most applications use degree 70 or higher for precision LEO. (2) Atmospheric drag: varies by factor 100 with solar activity; dominant perturbation below 800 km. Drag = 0.5 × ρ × Cd × A/m × v_rel^2. (3) Lunar gravity: 10^-5 to 10^-6 of Earth gravity, varies with Moon position. (4) Solar gravity: similar magnitude to lunar. (5) Solar radiation pressure (SRP): F = P_rad × C_r × A/m where P_rad = 4.56×10^-6 N/m^2 at 1 AU. Satellite area-to-mass ratio critical. Shadow transitions cause discontinuities. (6) Earth radiation pressure: reflected sunlight (albedo) and thermal emission. (7) Ocean and Earth tides: modify geopotential, important for altimetry missions. (8) Relativistic effects (geodetic precession): ~0.03 m/day for LEO. (9) Satellite self-force (thermal radiation, outgassing): small but relevant for high-precision missions."},
    {"id":"orbital-mechanics-lagrange-001","domain":"orbital_mechanics","title":"Lagrange Points and Halo Orbits","source":"Szebehely, Theory of Orbits; Richardson 1980","text":"The circular restricted three-body problem (CR3BP) has 5 equilibrium points (Lagrange points) in the rotating reference frame: L1: between Earth and Moon (or Sun and Earth), ~61,500 km from Moon (or 1,491,000 km from Earth at Sun-Earth L1). Unstable equilibrium. L2: beyond Moon (or Earth from Sun), ~61,500 km past Moon (or 1,491,000 km). Webb Space Telescope at Sun-Earth L2. L3: opposite Moon/Sun, behind Earth. L4/L5: equilateral triangle with primaries, 60° ahead/behind Moon. Trojan asteroids at Sun-Jupiter L4/L5 (>7,000 known). Stable equilibrium. Halo orbits: 3D periodic orbits near L1/L2 using non-linear out-of-plane motion. SOHO, WIND, ACE at Sun-Earth L1. ISEE-3, GENESIS, WMAP at Sun-Earth L2 (south). Webb, Gaia at Sun-Earth L2 (north). Near-Rectilinear Halo Orbit (NRHO): highly elliptical orbit near L2, stable due to gravitational perturbations. Selected for Lunar Gateway station. Advantages: near-continuous lunar surface visibility, moderate delta-V for lunar landing from NRHO."},
    {"id":"orbit-visualization-001","domain":"orbital_mechanics","title":"Orbital Visualization and Coverage Analysis","source":"Wertz, Space Mission Engineering; AGI STK documentation","text":"Orbit visualization and analysis tools: Celestrak: free online TLE data, satellite tracking. Heavens-Above: visual pass predictions for named satellites. N2YO: real-time satellite tracking with maps. STK (Ansys Systems Tool Kit / AGI): industry standard for mission analysis, coverage analysis, conjunction analysis. GMAT (NASA open-source): trajectory optimization, mission design. Poliastro (Python): open-source orbital mechanics library, visualization with Plotly. Skyfield (Python): high-accuracy planet and satellite positions. PyEphem (Python): simpler ephemeris tool. Coverage analysis metrics: field of regard (FOR): region accessible by satellite given gimbal limits; field of view (FOV): region imaged at any instant; access interval: time windows when target is in FOR; response time: time from tasking to first access; revisit time: time between access intervals. Example: single Sentinel-2 satellite at 786 km, 56° inclination achieves 5-day revisit at equator, 2-3 days at mid-latitudes with 290 km swath width."},
    # SATELLITE CONSTELLATIONS (additional)
    {"id":"constellation-design-001","domain":"satellite_constellations","title":"Constellation Design Optimization","source":"Wertz, Space Mission Engineering; Walker 1977","text":"Constellation design trades coverage, revisit time, and cost. Walker constellations: described as i:t/p/f where i=inclination, t=total satellites, p=number of planes, f=relative spacing factor (0 to p-1). Walker Delta pattern: optimal for global coverage. Example: GPS = 55°:24/6/2 (Kepler Walker). Rosette constellations: polar or near-polar orbits, optimized for high-latitude coverage. Coverage metrics: area coverage rate (km^2/s), global revisit time, response time. Coverage vs. altitude: higher altitude → larger footprint (single satellite covers more area) but higher launch cost, longer signal delay. LEO at 700 km: half-angle ~60°, ground footprint radius ~7,700 km. Mega-constellation design drivers: (1) continuous coverage requires multiple satellites visible simultaneously from any point (elevation > minimum E_min); (2) Starlink requires ~4-6 satellites visible at E_min=25°; (3) propellant for collision avoidance and disposal; (4) launch rate (hundreds/year for replenishment); (5) frequency coordination complexity grows with satellite count."},
    {"id":"constellation-operations-001","domain":"satellite_constellations","title":"Mega-Constellation Operations","source":"SpaceX; OneWeb operational documentation; public data","text":"Operating thousands of satellites requires highly automated systems. Key operational challenges: (1) Autonomous fault management: satellite must autonomously safe itself and respond to ground contact loss. State machine with transitions: safe mode → standby → operational. (2) Over-the-air updates: software patches deployed to thousands of satellites simultaneously, with staged rollout and rollback capability. (3) Collision avoidance at scale: automated CDM processing and maneuver execution. Starlink evaluates hundreds of CDMs per day. (4) Frequency management: dynamic spectrum sharing between beams. (5) Ground network scaling: thousands of ground stations or inter-satellite links to route traffic. (6) Handover: user terminal must track multiple satellites, seamless handoff between beams and satellites. (7) Gateway architecture: 5-10 ground gateways provide internet backhaul. Capacity allocation: onboard beam forming directs capacity to demand centers. Starlink uses phased array antennas (both satellite and user terminal), dynamically steering multiple beams."},
    {"id":"constellation-economic-001","domain":"satellite_constellations","title":"Satellite Constellation Economics","source":"Bryce Space Technology; public company data; market analysis","text":"Satellite constellation economic models: Starlink revenue model: consumer broadband ($120/month residential, $500 installation), maritime ($5,000/month), aviation (Starlink Aviation), cellular direct-to-device (T-Mobile partnership). Revenue target: $10-15B/year at full deployment. CapEx: ~$10B initial investment estimated. Unit economics: Starlink V2 Mini satellite cost ~$500K per unit at scale (SpaceX claim); launch cost ~$1M per satellite on Falcon 9 rideshare. OneWeb revenue model: enterprise, government, maritime. Revenue 2023: ~$500M. Investment: ~$2.4B raised. Amazon Kuiper: $10B investment committed, FCC license for 3,236 satellites. First commercial service 2024. Telesat Lightspeed: 298 satellites, Canadian government partnership, ~$5B capital. Key insight: LEO broadband economics favor scale — fixed launch and ground infrastructure costs spread over more users. Starlink has advantage of vertical integration (SpaceX makes rocket, satellite, user terminal). Competition from fiber and 5G in urban areas limits addressable market to remote/maritime/aviation."},
    # LAUNCH VEHICLES (additional)
    {"id":"launch-propulsion-001","domain":"launch_vehicles","title":"Launch Vehicle Propulsion Systems","source":"Sutton, Rocket Propulsion Elements; launch vehicle documentation","text":"Launch vehicle propulsion systems and performance: Solid rocket motors (SRMs): aluminum powder fuel + ammonium perchlorate oxidizer (APCP), Isp ~260-295 s. High thrust (MN range), simple, reliable, cannot throttle or restart. Used for strap-on boosters (SLS, Ariane 6) and first stages (Vega, Minotaur). Liquid engines: RP-1/LOX (Falcon 9 Merlin: Isp 311 s vac), LH2/LOX (RS-25 Space Shuttle Main Engine: Isp 453 s vac; Vulcain: Isp 433 s vac), MMH/N2O4 (storable, Isp 340 s), LCH4/LOX (Raptor: Isp 380 s vac SL / 380 sea-level). Advantages of LH2: highest Isp of chemical propellants (450+ s vacuum) but low density requires large tanks. RP-1: high density, good Isp, easy handling. Methalox (LCH4/LOX): higher Isp than RP-1, better than LH2 for density, Martian in-situ resource utilization (ISRU) possible. Turbo-pump vs electric pump-fed: SpaceX Merlin uses turbopump; Rocket Lab Rutherford uses electric pump (simpler, faster development, less efficient). Engine-out capability: Falcon 9 can land successfully with 2 of 9 engines out."},
    {"id":"launch-trajectory-001","domain":"launch_vehicles","title":"Launch Trajectory and Ascent Phases","source":"Turner, Rocket and Spacecraft Propulsion; SMAD","text":"Launch vehicle ascent profile: Liftoff: vertical thrust exceeds vehicle weight. Pitch-over (gravity turn): vehicle tilts toward trajectory azimuth shortly after liftoff, using natural aerodynamic gravity turn or programmed pitch. Max-Q: maximum dynamic pressure = 0.5 × ρ × v^2. Typically occurs at 12-16 km altitude, 50-60 seconds after launch. Engines throttle down at Max-Q to reduce structural loads. For Falcon 9: Max-Q ~30 kPa at ~12 km. MECO (Main Engine Cut-Off): first stage burnout; for Falcon 9 Block 5 at ~75 km, ~162 s. Stage separation: explosive bolts sever inter-stage; hot staging (Falcon 9) or cold staging. Stage 2 ignition: sustainer burn to orbit. Parking orbit: low energy orbit for checkout before upper stage burn to target orbit. GTO (Geostationary Transfer Orbit): perigee at LEO altitude (~185 km), apogee at GEO (35,786 km), period ~10.5 hours. Spacecraft performs apogee kick burn to circularize. Direct injection: some vehicles inject directly to final orbit (LV upper stage handles all burns)."},
    {"id":"launch-smallsat-001","domain":"launch_vehicles","title":"Small Satellite Launch Options","source":"Bryce Space Technology; Rocket Lab; various providers","text":"Small satellite launch landscape: Dedicated small launch vehicles: Rocket Lab Electron (300 kg to LEO, $7.5M), Firefly Alpha (1,000 kg to LEO, ~$15M), ABL RS1 (1,350 kg), Relativity Terran 1 (failed qualification), Astra Rocket 3 (discontinued). Indian small launchers: ISRO SSLV (500 kg to LEO, $5M), Agnikul Cosmos Agnibaan, Skyroot Vikram. Chinese small launchers: Kuaizhou (KZ-1A, 300 kg, solid), Galactic Energy Ceres-1 (solid, 350 kg), CAS Space Kinetica-1. European: Isar Aerospace (1,000 kg), PLD Space Miura 5 (300 kg), Orbex Prime (180 kg). Rideshare options: SpaceX Transporter (Falcon 9 SSO rideshare, $5,500/kg), Exolaunch (integration + deployment services), D-Orbit ION satellite carrier (on-orbit deployment). Trade-offs: dedicated launch: custom orbit, full control, higher cost per kg; rideshare: lower cost, fixed orbit (usually SSO 525 km), shared risk, waiting for manifest. Market trend: small sats (<500 kg) increasingly use rideshare due to cost. Very small (cubesat, <10 kg) almost exclusively rideshare."},
    # SPACECRAFT OPERATIONS (additional)
    {"id":"spacecraft-anomaly-001","domain":"spacecraft_operations","title":"Spacecraft Anomaly Management","source":"NASA GSFC operations documentation; ESA operations experience","text":"Spacecraft anomaly management process: Safe mode entry: satellite transitions to fault protection mode when off-nominal conditions detected (power deficit, thermal limits exceeded, attitude errors, communication loss). Safe mode typically: body-pointed toward Sun, minimal power consumption, continuous transmitting on low-gain antenna (LGA) at low data rate, telemetry beaconing. Recovery: ground team analyzes fault, uploads commands via LGA, gradually restores normal operations. Common anomaly types: (1) Power anomalies: battery depth-of-discharge exceeded, solar array failure, PDU failure; (2) Attitude anomalies: star tracker track loss, gyroscope bias, reaction wheel bearing failure; (3) Thermal anomalies: heater failure, louver stuck, shadow unexpectedly; (4) Software anomalies: software exception, memory corruption (SEU), command sequence error; (5) Propulsion: thruster stuck-open, propellant leak, pressurant loss; (6) Communications: RF chain failure, antenna deployment failure. Anomaly resolution team: operations, systems engineering, subsystem experts. Fault tree analysis (FTA) and failure modes effects analysis (FMEA) prepare teams for known failure modes."},
    {"id":"spacecraft-navigation-001","domain":"spacecraft_operations","title":"Spacecraft Navigation and Tracking","source":"Montenbruck & Gill; JPL navigation documentation","text":"Spacecraft navigation (radiometric tracking): Range measurement: two-way coherent ranging using uplink/downlink signals. Range precision: 1-10 m (standard); 1 cm with VLBI. Range-rate (Doppler): measured from carrier frequency shift. Precision: 0.1-1 mm/s. Angular measurements: Very Long Baseline Interferometry (VLBI) — two ground stations measure arrival time difference → angular position of spacecraft relative to quasar catalog. Precision: 10 nrad. Delta Differential One-way Range (ΔDOR): differential VLBI measurement between spacecraft and nearby quasar → angular precision ~2 nrad. Navigation data products: orbit determination solution (state vector + covariance), trajectory reconstruction, orbit prediction. DSN tracking data types: 1-way, 2-way, 3-way Doppler and range. Navigation accuracy for interplanetary missions: position accuracy at Mars ~1 km (3σ) during approach. ISS position: determined by GPS receivers onboard, accurate to ~10 m. For LEO collision avoidance: GPS-based navigation gives ~1-3 m accuracy."},
    {"id":"spacecraft-reliability-001","domain":"spacecraft_operations","title":"Spacecraft Reliability and Redundancy","source":"Fortescue, Spacecraft Systems Engineering; MIL-HDBK-338","text":"Spacecraft reliability design: Failure modes: random hardware failures, wearout (end-of-life), design errors, manufacturing defects, environment effects (radiation, thermal cycling, vacuum). Redundancy types: (1) Cold redundancy: backup unit powered off until primary fails (zero standby failure rate but longer switchover time); (2) Hot redundancy: both units running simultaneously (higher power, immediate failover); (3) Functional redundancy: different subsystems can perform same function (attitude determination from star tracker or magnetometer). Reliability metrics: MTBF (mean time between failures), reliability function R(t) = exp(-t/MTBF) for exponential distribution. Typical component MTBF values: reaction wheel ~100,000 hours, star tracker ~150,000 hours, transponder ~200,000 hours. Mission reliability: for 15-year GEO satellite with 95% reliability requirement, each subsystem must achieve R(15yr) ≥ 0.95^(1/n). Redundancy level 2 (N+1): adds ~15-20% mass and cost for critical components. Mass allocation for redundancy: typically 10-20% of subsystem mass."},
    # SPACE SITUATIONAL AWARENESS (additional)
    {"id":"ssa-sensors-optical-001","domain":"space_situational_awareness","title":"Optical SSA Sensors and Telescopes","source":"ESA GEODSS; Lincoln Laboratory; 18th SpCS","text":"Optical telescopes for space surveillance detect sunlight reflected from satellites and debris. Conditions for visibility: satellite must be illuminated (usually within ~2 hours after sunset or before sunrise when satellite is in sunlight but ground is dark); clear sky; magnitude brighter than telescope limit. Brightness (magnitude) depends on: object size, albedo (reflectivity 0.05-0.5), phase angle (sun-object-observer angle), range. A 10 cm sphere at 400 km with albedo 0.2 at favorable phase: ~+7 magnitude (detectable with binoculars in ideal conditions). A 1 m satellite at GEO: ~+12 magnitude. Telescope systems: GEODSS (Ground-based Electro-Optical Deep Space Surveillance): 3 sites (Maui, New Mexico, Diego Garcia), 1 m telescopes, detects GEO objects to magnitude ~16.5 (0.3 m at GEO). Space Surveillance Telescope (SST): 3.5 m, wide field of view, moved to Australia. Boeing SBSS (Space-Based Space Surveillance) satellite: optical telescope in LEO for GEO surveillance. Maui Space Surveillance Complex: 3.67 m AEOS telescope, laser ranging, adaptive optics imaging."},
    {"id":"ssa-data-sharing-001","domain":"space_situational_awareness","title":"SSA Data Sharing and International Cooperation","source":"IADC; UN COPUOS; Space-Track.org","text":"SSA data sharing mechanisms: Space-Track.org: US Space Command provides TLE data, CDMs, decay predictions to registered users (free registration). Contains basic unclassified data on ~27,000 objects. Approximately 7,000 registered users in 150 countries. European SST (Space Surveillance and Tracking) Programme: ESA/EU consortium provides SSA data to European operators. Space Data Association (SDA): industry organization for satellite operator data sharing. Members include Intelsat, SES, Eutelsat, Inmarsat, others. Shares precision orbital data and CDMs among members on reciprocal basis. China Space Debris Monitoring and Application Center (CMSA): provides Chinese catalog data, some sharing with international partners. IADC data sharing: agency-to-agency data exchange for calibration and validation. Key tension: military space assets data classification vs. commercial operator need for collision avoidance data. US DoD policy: share basic public catalog but withhold high-accuracy government satellite data. Commercial operators: prefer independent commercial SSA to avoid dependence on US government data."},
    {"id":"ssa-risk-assessment-001","domain":"space_situational_awareness","title":"Orbital Risk Assessment Methods","source":"Klinkrad, Space Debris; ESA Debris Risk Assessment","text":"Long-term orbital risk assessment methods: DEBRIS risk index: combines object mass × collision cross-section × collision probability per unit time. Objects with highest risk index are priority for ADR. Fragmentation models: NASA Standard Breakup Model (SBM): predicts number, mass, area, and velocity distribution of fragments from hypervelocity collisions or explosions. Input: combined mass, impact velocity, impact angle. Output: statistical fragment distribution. Used in LEGEND and ORDEM models. Collision avoidance statistics: for 27,000 tracked objects, ~50 high-priority events (Pc > 1e-5) per day across all objects. Expected collision rate: 1 per decade between catalogued objects (current era), growing. Insurance market: GEO satellite insurance: ~0.5-1% of satellite value per year for in-orbit risk. Typical GEO satellite value: $200-400M, premium $1-4M/year. LEO constellation insurance: complex actuarial challenge due to volume and collision cascading risk. Lloyd's of London and AXA Space primary underwriters. Self-insurance common for operators with large fleets (SpaceX, OneWeb)."},
    # STANDARDS AND PROTOCOLS (additional)
    {"id":"standards-debris-removal-001","domain":"standards_protocols","title":"Debris Removal Standards and Certification","source":"ISO; ESA Clean Space; European Space Policy","text":"Standards for active debris removal certification: ECSS-E-HB-32-25A (Spacecraft mechanical loads analysis handbook): structural requirements for ADR capture mechanisms. ISO 16290 (Space systems — Definition of the Technology Readiness Levels, TRL): ADR technologies typically at TRL 5-7, full mission TRL 9. CCSDS Proximity-1 (211.0-B-5): standard data link protocol for inter-spacecraft communication during rendezvous. ESA HERMES project: heritage from Automated Transfer Vehicle (ATV) docking expertise. JAXA proximity operations experience from HTV. Key standards gaps (identified by ESA): no international standard for rendezvous interface geometry (harpoon target vs. robotic arm vs. magnetic docking); no certification requirement for ADR capability; liability issues — who is responsible if ADR mission causes debris? Liability Convention (1972): launching state responsible for damage caused by space objects. ADR: if the chaser damages the debris during capture, the chaser state may be liable. International registration required for satellites (Registration Convention 1975). Future standards development focus: debris characterization, interface standardization, liability framework."},
    {"id":"standards-tle-generation-001","domain":"standards_protocols","title":"TLE Generation and Quality Standards","source":"18th SpCS; Celestrak documentation; Spacetrack API","text":"TLE quality and generation standards: US Space Command TLE generation process: (1) Sensor data collected from SSN sensors (radar and optical); (2) Observation preprocessing: ionospheric correction, tropospheric refraction, station displacement; (3) Batch least-squares differential correction to fit SGP4 mean elements; (4) Quality control: residual analysis, consistency with previous orbit; (5) TLE output formatted and uploaded to Space-Track. TLE data quality metrics: Observed minus Computed (O-C) residuals (typical 50-200 m range residual for well-tracked LEO); object age (time since last observation update). TLE quality flags on Space-Track: RCSSIZE (large, medium, small, unknown), OBJECT_TYPE (payload, rocket body, debris, unknown), ANALYST_NOTEBOOK for analyst notes on problematic objects. Independent TLE generation: Celestrak provides SATCAT data supplementing Space-Track. SOCRATES conjunction tool. Private operators: many operators generate precision TLEs from GPS receivers aboard their satellites — these are more accurate but not shared publicly. AST SpaceMobile, Planet Labs use GPS-derived orbits for conjunction screening."},
    {"id":"standards-space-law-001","domain":"standards_protocols","title":"Space Law and Liability Framework","source":"UN Outer Space Treaty 1967; Liability Convention 1972; Registration Convention 1975","text":"International space law framework: Outer Space Treaty (OST, 1967): fundamental treaty signed by 111 states. Key provisions: space is the 'province of all mankind'; no national appropriation; states responsible for national activities including non-governmental; no weapons of mass destruction in space; Moon and celestial bodies for peaceful purposes. Liability Convention (1972): absolute liability for damage on Earth surface or to aircraft; fault liability for in-space damage. US is responsible for SpaceX debris damage under absolute liability. Registration Convention (1975): states must register spacecraft and notify UN. States maintain national registries; UN maintains public registry. Moon Agreement (1979): resources of Moon are 'common heritage of mankind'; limited ratification, not signed by major space powers. National legislation: US Commercial Space Launch Competitiveness Act (2015) — US citizens may own resources they extract in space. Luxembourg Space Resources Law (2017). UAE, Luxembourg, Japan, Australia have similar laws. Recent developments: Artemis Accords — bilateral agreements between US and partner countries for lunar activities; 46+ signatories."},
    # SPACE ENVIRONMENT (additional)
    {"id":"space-environment-debris-flux-001","domain":"space_environment","title":"Debris and Micrometeoroid Flux Models","source":"NASA ORDEM 3.1; ESA MASTER-8; ECSS-E-ST-10-04C","text":"Orbital debris flux models for spacecraft design: NASA ORDEM 3.1 (Orbital Debris Engineering Model): provides debris flux as function of: altitude, inclination, object size (0.01 mm to 10 m), direction (ram, anti-ram, zenith, etc.), year. Validated against impact data from Space Shuttle windows and exposed surfaces (Long Duration Exposure Facility, LDEF). Input: orbit parameters, time period, minimum particle size of concern. Output: flux (impacts/m^2/year) and cumulative fluence. ESA MASTER-8 (Meteoroid and Space Debris Terrestrial Environment Reference): similar capability, uses ESA sensor network data for validation. Inputs same as ORDEM. Micrometeoroid models: NASA Meteoroid Engineering Model (MEM), ESA IMEM. Sporadic background + stream contributions. Impact damage modeling: crater diameter, spall diameter, penetration depth as function of particle diameter, density, velocity. Application: spacecraft MMOD risk assessment required for: ISS modules (NASA SP-8042), crewed vehicles (RFP requirement), high-value mission-critical components. LDEF (Long Duration Exposure Facility, NASA, 1984-1990): 69 months in orbit, returned 57 trays of material. Provided foundational debris flux measurements."},
    {"id":"space-environment-charging-001","domain":"space_environment","title":"Spacecraft Charging and ESD","source":"NASA GSFC SP-8089; ECSS-E-ST-20-06C; Hastings & Garrett","text":"Spacecraft charging occurs when the spacecraft accumulates net electric charge from the space plasma environment. Two types: Surface charging: occurs in all orbits. Ambient plasma (ions and electrons) impinge on surfaces; differential currents cause surface potential variations. GEO: substorm electrons (10-100 keV) charge shadowed surfaces to -20,000 V; illuminated surfaces charge positively from photoelectric emission. LEO: ionospheric plasma densities prevent large charging. Secondary electrons and photoelectrons modify charging. Deep dielectric charging (bulk charging): high-energy electrons (>100 keV) penetrate surface materials and deposit charge in interior dielectrics. Typically requires days of exposure. Causes internal electrostatic discharge (IESD) — more damaging than surface discharge. Most damaging in outer radiation belt (GEO, MEO). Mitigation: design rules (avoid surface potential differences > 100 V), grounding, conductive coatings (ITO — Indium Tin Oxide), resistive coatings for thermal blankets. ESD testing: ECSS-E-ST-20-06C requires arc testing of materials. Charging models: NASCAP-2K (NASA), MUSCAT (JAXA), COULOMB-2 (Onera)."},
    {"id":"atomic-oxygen-001","domain":"space_environment","title":"Atomic Oxygen Effects on Spacecraft","source":"NASA LDEF data; ECSS-E-ST-10-04C","text":"Atomic oxygen (AO) is the dominant neutral species in LEO at 200-700 km altitude. Density peaks near 200 km, negligible above 700 km. AO flux at 400 km: ~10^15 atoms/cm^2/s for nadir-facing surface. AO effects on spacecraft materials: Oxidation and erosion: polymer films, carbon, silver, and osmium erode rapidly. Kapton (polyimide, common in MLI blankets): erosion rate ~3×10^-24 cm^3/atom. At ISS altitude (408 km) for 10 years: ~1 mm erosion of unprotected Kapton. Atomic oxygen erosion yields (cm^3/atom): carbon ~10^-24, silver ~10^-25, osmium very low, silicone ≈ 0 (forms protective silica layer), aluminum ≈ 0 (oxide layer). Protected materials: silicon dioxide (SiO2), Al2O3, gold. Testing: LDEF (Long Duration Exposure Facility) provided 5.8 years of AO exposure data. Ground testing: radio-frequency plasma systems and electron cyclotron resonance (ECR) plasma. AO also causes optical property changes: enhanced absorption in solar cells, changes in thermal control coatings. Design practice: protect exposed polymer films with SiO2 or Al coatings; avoid exposed silver interconnects."},
    # MISSION PLANNING (additional)
    {"id":"mission-requirements-001","domain":"mission_planning","title":"Mission Requirements Development","source":"NASA Systems Engineering Handbook; ECSS-M-ST-10C; SMAD","text":"Space mission requirements are hierarchical: Level 1 — Mission requirements: top-level performance (coverage, accuracy, capacity), cost, schedule constraints. Derived from customer/user needs. Level 2 — System requirements: system-level performance, interface, reliability, environmental. Level 3 — Subsystem requirements: derived from system requirements for each subsystem. Requirements characteristics: SMART (Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Traceable). Each requirement has a unique identifier, verification method (analysis, test, inspection, demonstration), and traceability to higher-level requirements. Key mission design parameters (from SMAD): orbit altitude → swath width; number of satellites → revisit time; aperture size → resolution; transmitted power → data rate; sensor wavelength → target detection. Requirements drivers for SSA missions: coverage: what fraction of LEO must be observed per day? Sensitivity: minimum object size detectable. Latency: how quickly must conjunction data reach operators? Reliability: what is the probability of mission success over 5 years?"},
    {"id":"mission-delta-v-budget-001","domain":"mission_planning","title":"Mission Delta-V Budget","source":"Wertz, Space Mission Engineering; SMAD","text":"Satellite mission delta-V budget accounts for all velocity changes during mission lifetime: Launch injection correction: 10-50 m/s to correct for launch vehicle dispersions. Orbit insertion: altitude and inclination corrections, 50-200 m/s. Station acquisition: phasing within constellation, 0-100 m/s. Station-keeping (LEO): altitude maintenance against drag. ΔV = D × t/m = (1/2 × ρ × Cd × A × v^2) × t/m. Example: 100 kg satellite at 500 km, Cd=2.2, A=0.01 m^2: ΔV ≈ 10 m/s/year. Station-keeping (GEO): N-S: ~50 m/s/year. E-W: ~2-5 m/s/year. Attitude control (desaturation): 1-5 m/s/year depending on disturbance torques. Collision avoidance: 0.5-2 m/s/event, 1-5 events/year for LEO constellation. Disposal: LEO reentry ΔV: 5-50 m/s; GEO graveyard: 10-15 m/s. Total example: 15-year GEO satellite: station-keeping = 750 m/s (N-S) + 60 m/s (E-W) + disposal 12 m/s ≈ 850 m/s. With Isp=340 s: propellant fraction 22%. For 3,000 kg dry mass: 850 kg propellant. Total launch mass: 3,850 kg."},
    # ISS AND HUMAN SPACEFLIGHT
    {"id":"human-spaceflight-001","domain":"space_missions","title":"Human Spaceflight Programs","source":"NASA; ESA Human Spaceflight; Roscosmos","text":"Current human spaceflight programs (2024-2026): ISS: crew rotation via SpaceX Crew Dragon (NASA Commercial Crew Program — Boeing Starliner completed first crewed flight 2024 but returned crew via Dragon due to propulsion concerns) and Soyuz (Russia). Average crew rotation every 6 months. Commercial Low Earth Orbit Destinations (CLD): NASA funding Axiom Space (Ax Station, modules to attach to ISS then detach as independent station), Starlab (Voyager Space/Nanoracks), Northrop Grumman commercial station. China: Tiangong Space Station: core module Tianhe (2021), lab modules Wentian and Mengtian (2022). Crew: 3 taikonauts. Regular crew rotation via Shenzhou. 25-year planned lifetime. India: Gaganyaan: India's first crewed spaceflight program. LVM3 (GSLV Mk III) launch vehicle. 4-member crew module, 3-day mission to LEO. Uncrewed test 2023; planned crewed mission 2025. Artemis: first crewed lunar landing planned 2026 (Artemis III). Commercial suborbital: Blue Origin New Shepard (crew flights), Virgin Galactic VSS Unity (retired 2023), Virgin Galactic Delta-class vehicle."},
    {"id":"iss-operations-detail-001","domain":"space_missions","title":"ISS Operations and Research","source":"NASA ISS Program; ESA Columbus; JAXA Kibo","text":"ISS operations details: Orbit: 408 km altitude, 51.6° inclination. Reboost: performed by Progress resupply vehicles or European ATV (retired), servicing module main engines, or visiting vehicles. ~2 km/s ΔV per year to compensate drag. Attitude control: control moment gyroscopes (CMGs) primary; thrusters for CMG desaturation. Power: 8 solar array wings, 2,500 m^2 total area, 160 kW peak. Batteries: NiH2 replaced by Li-ion (2017-2021 upgrade). Life support: Water Recovery System (WRS) recovers 90%+ of water from urine and condensate. Oxygen Generation Assembly (OGA) electrolyzes water. CO2 Removal Assembly (CDRA) uses zeolite molecular sieve. EVA: extravehicular activity (spacewalk) approximately once per month. US EVAs use EMU (Extravehicular Mobility Unit, $12M suit); Russian EVAs use Orlan. Research: ~400 science investigations ongoing; key areas: microgravity biology, materials science, human physiology, Earth observation. SpaceX Cargo Dragon and Northrop Grumman Cygnus provide unmanned resupply."},
    # ADDITIONAL ORBITAL PROPAGATION
    {"id":"deep-space-propagation-001","domain":"orbital_propagation","title":"Deep Space Orbit Propagation","source":"Vallado; Bate Mueller White; Chapront-Touze","text":"Deep space orbit propagation uses different models than near-Earth. For lunar and interplanetary trajectories: patched-conic approximation: divide mission into sphere of influence (SOI) segments, use two-body problem within each SOI. Earth SOI: ~925,000 km. Moon SOI: ~66,200 km. Accurate propagation requires N-body numerical integration: EOM: r̈ = -μ_Earth/r^3 × r + Σ μ_n × (r_n - r)/|r_n - r|^3 - μ_n × r_n/r_n^3 for each perturbing body n. Software tools: JPL Horizons: N-body ephemeris for solar system objects. DE440 planetary ephemeris: precise positions of Sun, planets, Moon. STK Astrogator: advanced propagation and maneuver optimization. GMAT: NASA open-source tool for trajectory design and optimization. Trajectory design: for interplanetary missions, delta-V is minimized using Hohmann-like transfers between planets, timed to align planetary positions (launch windows repeat every synodic period). Venus-Earth-Earth Gravity Assist (VEEGA) used by Galileo to reach Jupiter. Pork chop plots: contours of C3 (excess hyperbolic energy) as function of launch/arrival date."},
    # ADVANCED SSA TOPICS
    {"id":"ssa-machine-learning-001","domain":"space_situational_awareness","title":"Machine Learning in SSA","source":"DARPA; AFRL; academic publications","text":"Machine learning applications in Space Situational Awareness: (1) Orbit determination: neural networks learn mapping from sparse observations to orbit state, faster than iterative differential correction for known object types. (2) Object classification: ML classifiers distinguish payload, rocket body, debris from radar signatures (RCS variation vs. aspect angle, tumble rates). Convolutional neural networks on radar cross-section time series. (3) Maneuver detection: detecting when an active satellite performs an uncoordinated maneuver (large TLE residuals, anomalous RCS changes). Crucial for avoiding false positives in conjunction screening. (4) Conjunction probability improvement: Gaussian process models for atmospheric density uncertainty, improving Pc accuracy. (5) Sensor scheduling: reinforcement learning to optimize radar dwell times and telescope pointing for maximum catalog maintenance. (6) Anomaly detection: isolation forest, LSTM autoencoders for spacecraft telemetry anomaly detection. (7) Population model inference: using measurement data to infer untracked debris population statistics. Key challenge: training data scarcity (limited collision events), class imbalance (few dangerous events vs. millions of non-events)."},
    {"id":"ssa-cislunar-001","domain":"space_situational_awareness","title":"Cislunar Space Domain Awareness","source":"DARPA Cislunar Highway Patrol; AFRL; NASA CAPSTONE","text":"Cislunar space (Earth-Moon system, inside Moon's orbit) presents new SSA challenges as human activity increases. Volume: cislunar space is ~1 billion km^3 — orders of magnitude larger than LEO/GEO surveillance areas. Current tracking capability: existing ground radars effective to ~36,000 km (GEO); Moon is at 384,400 km. Optical telescopes can track large objects to lunar distance but sky coverage is limited. Challenges: (1) No persistent coverage; (2) Trajectories are 3-body dynamics, not simple SGP4; (3) Moon's shadow creates tracking gaps; (4) Objects in Lagrange point orbits (halo, NRO) have very different observability. DARPA Cislunar Highway Patrol System (CHPS): proposed constellation of satellites in cislunar orbit for wide-area space domain awareness. AFRL VICTUS NOX/HAZE: rapid-response satellite demonstrations for cislunar. NASA CAPSTONE (2022): CubeSat in lunar NRHO demonstrating navigation and communication. Growing activity: Artemis program, commercial lunar services (Astrobotic Peregrine, Intuitive Machines IM-1/IM-2), China Chang'e missions, commercial communication relays (Nokia, CommStar). Cislunar SSA requires new orbital models, sensor architectures, and international coordination."},
]

if __name__ == "__main__":
    print(f"Total corpus entries: {len(CORPUS)}")
